# A lane-wise select one of whose arms is zero, which is an `and` - §41.4 of test/bench/findings.md.
#
# The baseline's select is `movaps xmm0, mask ; pblendvb`, two instructions and the xmm0 clobber that
# `FormVSelectBlend` argues for. A zero arm makes it one bitwise instruction with no clobber at all,
# and - the half that is worth more - takes the last reader off the zero, so the register that was
# holding it across whatever loop the select is in goes back to the pool. What to look for:
#
#   - **`pand`/`andps` where the zero is the false arm**, the mask being the one that is *kept*, and
#     **`pandn`/`andnps` where it is the true arm**, `pandn` computing `~lhs & rhs` so that the
#     complement is in the opcode rather than in an instruction in front of it.
#   - **the domain follows the value and not the mask.** A masked float vector takes `andps`, whose
#     result is read back in the domain it was produced in; a mask lane is a truth value rather than
#     a float, so asking the *mask*'s type would put every one of these back in the integer domain.
#   - **no `pxor` anywhere.** The zero is gone rather than moved: nothing reads it, so the splat that
#     built it goes with the select.
#
# `VectorVex.lower` is where the same rewrite is read under VEX, where what it replaces is one
# `vpblendvb` rather than two instructions and is still worth the register.

# The false arm, which is `value & mask`. Integer lanes, so `pand` - which has no lane width, one
# instruction serving every vector and every mask.
keepInt(%a: i32x4, %b: i32x4, %v: i32x4): i32x4 {
    {
        %m = cmp_eq %a, %b: m32x4
        %z = vsplat 0: i32x4
        %r = select %m, %v, %z: i32x4
        ret %r
    }
}

# The true arm, which is `~mask & value` - one instruction, and the destination is tied to the *mask*
# because that is the operand `pandn` complements.
dropInt(%a: i32x4, %b: i32x4, %v: i32x4): i32x4 {
    {
        %m = cmp_eq %a, %b: m32x4
        %z = vsplat 0: i32x4
        %r = select %m, %z, %v: i32x4
        ret %r
    }
}

# The same pair over float lanes, which take the float-domain rows: `andps` and `andnps` do to these
# bits exactly what `pand` and `pandn` do, and differ in the forwarding domain the result is read
# from. This is the shape `clipped` in test/bench/programs/VecFloat.yana has in its inner loop.
keepFloat(%a: f32x4, %b: f32x4, %v: f32x4): f32x4 {
    {
        %m = cmp_lt %a, %b: m32x4
        %f = imm 0.0f: Float
        %z = vsplat %f: f32x4
        %r = select %m, %v, %z: f32x4
        ret %r
    }
}

dropFloat(%a: f64x2, %b: f64x2, %v: f64x2): f64x2 {
    {
        %m = cmp_lt %a, %b: m64x2
        %d = imm 0.0: Double
        %z = vsplat %d: f64x2
        %r = select %m, %z, %v: f64x2
        ret %r
    }
}

# A select whose arms are two ordinary values, which is left exactly as it was: the rewrite is about
# the zero rather than about the select, and this is the row `FormVSelectBlend` is still selected for.
bothArms(%a: i32x4, %b: i32x4, %v: i32x4, %w: i32x4): i32x4 {
    {
        %m = cmp_eq %a, %b: m32x4
        %r = select %m, %v, %w: i32x4
        ret %r
    }
}
